.Dd October 26, 2026
.Dt NVCX 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nvcx
.Nd simulate AMD/Xilinx xsim cores inside the NVC VHDL simulator
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------
.\" Synopsis
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm nvc
.Fl Fl work Ns = Ns Ar nvcx:nvcx
.Fl a
.Pa nvcx.vhd
.Pp
.Nm nvc
.Fl Fl map Ns = Ns Ar nvcx:nvcx
.Fl Fl do
.Ar top
.Ar script.tcl
.Pp
.Nm nvc
.Fl Fl map Ns = Ns Ar nvcx:nvcx
.Fl r
.Fl Fl load
.Pa libnvcx.so
.Ar top
.Pp
.Bd -literal -offset indent
package require nvcx
nvcx::write_shell -component name -output file \e
    (-snapshot dir | -library lib ?-elab args?)
.Ed
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------
.\" Description
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
embeds the AMD/Xilinx xsim simulator inside
.Xr nvc 1 ,
so that cores NVC cannot simulate itself (IEEE 1735 encrypted IP, XPM
macros, and SystemVerilog beyond NVC's current support) can be part of an
otherwise ordinary NVC simulation.
.Pp
Each such core is declared as a VHDL component in the design and left
unbound.
.Nm
replaces it with a generated VHDL
.Em shell :
an entity of the same name and interface whose architecture instantiates
the
.Li nvcx.bridge
entity.
The bridge is bound, through a VHPI foreign attribute, to a plugin that
loads xsim's simulation kernel and the core's xsim snapshot, and exchanges
port values with NVC.
NVC simulates the rest of the design, which is free to use VHPI, PSL,
waveform dumping and the like as usual.
.Pp
.Nm
is not a program.
It consists of
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
the bridge entity's VHDL source,
.Pa nvcx.vhd ,
to be analysed into a library named
.Li nvcx ;
.It
a Tcl package,
.Li nvcx ,
whose
.Ic nvcx::write_shell
command, run under
.Nm nvc Fl Fl do ,
writes each core's shell and elaborates its snapshot;
.It
a VHPI plugin,
.Pa libnvcx.so ,
loaded with
.Nm nvc Fl Fl load ;
and
.It
a
.Xr pkg-config 1
file,
.Pa nvcx.pc ,
which locates the other three.
.El
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------
.\" Flow
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh FLOW
A simulation using
.Nm
elaborates the design twice: once with the cores' components unbound, so
that
.Nm
can find them, and again with their shells.
.Bl -enum
.It
Analyse the bridge entity into library
.Li nvcx ,
once:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ nvc --work=nvcx:nvcx -a $(pkg-config --variable=vhdl nvcx)
.Ed
.It
Analyse and elaborate the design.
NVC warns that the cores' components are unbound; this is expected.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ nvc --map=nvcx:nvcx -a tb.vhd -e tb
.Ed
.It
Run a Tcl script that calls
.Ic nvcx::write_shell
for each core, against the elaborated design:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ TCLLIBPATH=$(pkg-config --variable=tcldir nvcx) \e
      nvc --map=nvcx:nvcx --do tb shells.tcl
.Ed
.It
Analyse the shells and elaborate again:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ nvc --map=nvcx:nvcx -a *_shell.vhd -e tb
.Ed
.It
Run the simulation with the plugin loaded:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ nvc --map=nvcx:nvcx -r \e
      --load $(pkg-config --variable=plugin nvcx) tb
.Ed
.El
.Pp
Steps 3 and 4 need repeating only when a core, its interface or its
generics change.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------
.\" Tcl commands
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh TCL COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Xo
.Ic nvcx::write_shell
.Fl component Ar name
.Fl output Ar file
.Pq Fl snapshot Ar dir | Fl library Ar lib Op Fl elab Ar args
.Xc
Write the VHDL shell for the component
.Ar name
(case-insensitive, as declared in the design) to
.Ar file ,
and return
.Ar file .
The shell's generics and ports are taken from NVC's view of the
component's declaration, so the declaration must match the core's
interface.
It is an error if the elaborated design instantiates no component of that
name; the message lists the components it does instantiate.
Exactly one of
.Fl snapshot
and
.Fl library
must be given.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl snapshot Ar dir
Use a snapshot elaborated already by
.Ql xelab -dll ,
for example by a script from Vivado's
.Ic export_simulation .
.Ar dir
is the snapshot's directory,
.Pa xsim.dir/ Ns Ar name .
The core's generics were fixed when it was elaborated; the shell declares
them with the instantiation's values as defaults, and every instance must
leave them at those values.
.It Fl library Ar lib
Elaborate the snapshot
.Ar lib . Ns Ar name
with
.Ic xelab
once for each distinct set of generic values the design instantiates the
component with, passing them as
.Fl generic_top .
The shell selects the matching snapshot at run time.
Snapshots are cached between runs (see
.Sx FILES ) ,
so an unchanged design does not elaborate again.
This suits precompiled libraries such as
.Li xpm .
.It Fl elab Ar args
With
.Fl library
only: the arguments to pass to
.Xr xelab ,
as a Tcl list.
The default is
.Ql -L Ar lib .
.El
.El
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------
.\" Interfaces
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh SUPPORTED INTERFACES
Ports must have mode
.Li in
or
.Li out ;
.Li inout
and
.Li buffer
ports are not supported.
A port's type must be one of
.Li std_ulogic ,
.Li std_ulogic_vector ,
.Li signed ,
.Li unsigned ,
.Li bit ,
.Li bit_vector ,
.Li integer ,
.Li real
or
.Li boolean ,
or a subtype of one of these such as
.Li std_logic_vector .
Array widths come from the actual signals, so the shell declares array
ports unconstrained.
A port of a Verilog or SystemVerilog core must be a
.Li std_logic
or
.Li bit
scalar or array; four-state values pass through.
.Pp
Generics must be of type
.Li integer ,
.Li string ,
.Li real
or
.Li boolean .
Generic names are passed to
.Ic xelab
as declared, since Verilog parameter names are case-sensitive.
.Pp
Other components the design instantiates (a bound UNISIM primitive, for
instance) may use any types: only components named to
.Ic nvcx::write_shell
are checked.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------
.\" Timing
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh TIMING
NVC and the xsim kernel advance in lockstep, one delta cycle at a time.
At each delta, inputs NVC changed are written to the kernel, the kernel
runs one delta, and the outputs that changed are deposited for NVC's next
delta.
Each side therefore sees the other's assignments one delta after they were
made, as it would in a single simulator.
.Pp
Every NVC time at which the core runs must be a whole number of the
kernel's time precision; elaborate the core with a finer precision if
.Nm
reports otherwise.
All instances share one kernel.
.Pp
Reports and assertions from a core become NVC assertions of the same
severity, prefixed with the shell's instance name, so
.Nm nvc Fl Fl exit-severity
governs them as it does any other.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------
.\" Environment
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev XILINX_VIVADO
The Vivado installation whose simulator kernel and
.Ic xelab
are used, as set by Vivado's
.Pa settings64.sh .
Required.
A snapshot can only be simulated by the Vivado release that elaborated it;
.Nm
refuses a mismatch.
Vivado 2023.2 or later is required.
.It Ev NVCX_WORK
Directory holding the snapshot cache.
The default is
.Pa nvcx.work
in the current directory.
.It Ev TCLLIBPATH
Must include the Tcl package directory,
.Ql pkg-config --variable=tcldir nvcx ,
for
.Ql package require nvcx
to succeed, unless the package is installed in Tcl's default path.
.El
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------
.\" Files
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh FILES
Installed paths are given for a prefix of
.Pa /usr ;
.Xr pkg-config 1
reports the actual ones.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /usr/lib/nvcx/libnvcx.so
The VHPI plugin
.Pq Ql pkg-config --variable=plugin nvcx .
Other libraries in this directory are stand-ins for parts of Vivado and
are not loaded directly.
.It Pa /usr/share/nvcx/nvcx.vhd
The bridge entity
.Pq Ql pkg-config --variable=vhdl nvcx .
It is instantiated only by generated shells.
.It Pa /usr/share/nvcx/tcl/
The directory holding the
.Li nvcx
Tcl package
.Pq Ql pkg-config --variable=tcldir nvcx .
.It Pa /usr/lib/pkgconfig/nvcx.pc
The
.Xr pkg-config 1
file.
.It Pa $NVCX_WORK/snapshots/
Snapshots elaborated by
.Ic nvcx::write_shell Fl library ,
one directory per core and set of generics, keyed by the library, the
entity, the
.Ic xelab
arguments, the generic values and the Vivado release.
Each holds the
.Ic xelab
log,
.Pa xelab.log .
It is safe to delete; snapshots are elaborated again as needed.
.El
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------
.\" Diagnostics
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Nm
reports problems as NVC assertions prefixed with
.Ql nvcx: .
Most are failures that end the simulation.
One is a warning: if a shell's snapshot is missing, or the design
instantiates a core with generics no snapshot was elaborated for, the
instance becomes a black box with undriven outputs.
Re-run the script that wrote the shell.
.Pp
If the bridge entity is simulated without the plugin loaded, it fails with
.Dq foreign binding not resolved ;
add
.Fl Fl load
to the
.Nm nvc Fl r
command line.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------
.\" Examples
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh EXAMPLES
A script, executable thanks to its first line, that writes shells for an
encrypted IP core exported from Vivado and for an XPM macro:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#!/usr/bin/env -S nvc --std=2008 --map=nvcx:nvcx --map=xpm:xpm --do tb

package require nvcx

nvcx::write_shell -component hello_dds \e
    -output hello_dds_shell.vhd \e
    -snapshot export_sim/hello_dds/xsim/xsim.dir/hello_dds

nvcx::write_shell -component xpm_memory_sdpram \e
    -output xpm_memory_sdpram_shell.vhd \e
    -library xpm
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Pa hello
directory of the source distribution contains the complete example.
